  "verbalization": "Gambogenic Acid is shown to have a target relationship with Raji cells, which are then targeted by multiple compounds, including Diosmetin 7-O-Beta-D-Glucopyranosyl-(1->2)-Beta-D-Glucopyranosyl-(1->2)-[Alpha-L-Rhamnopyranosyl-(1->6)]-Beta-D-Glucopyranoside and its stereoisomer (1R,3R,4S,7S,9S,11S,13R,14R,15R)-4,11,13,14-tetrahydroxy-7-[(2Z,4R)-4-(hydroxymethyl)hex-2-en-2-yl]-3,9,15-trimethoxy-12,12-dimethyl-6,17-dioxabicyclo[11.3.1]heptadecan-5-one. All these compounds are linked to HepG2, a human liver cell line, suggesting a potential mechanism by which Gambogenic Acid may exert its effects on HepG2. The evidence points to a possible pathway where Gambogenic Acid targets Raji cells, which are then targeted by various bioactive compounds, ultimately leading to a target effect on HepG2. The shared target relationship between Gambogenic Acid and HepG2 implies a possible mechanism involving the interaction of these compounds with cellular pathways, though the exact molecular mechanism remains to be elucidated.",
